onal liability, shifting the burden of error onto the hospitals and banks that adopt these un-inspected optimizations.\n\nHistorical precedents in the telecommunications and aviation sectors show that efficiency gains invariably induce higher total resource consumption rather than conservation. The Jevons paradox applies directly to algorithmic optimization, where lower compute cost per token expands total query volume across enterprise applications. Regulators at the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission and state public utility commissions now face interconnection requests from data center operators that exceed regional generation capacity. No amount of software scaling can repeal the laws of thermodynamics or eliminate the organizational friction of institutional adoption.",
